From: postmarketOS <bramble@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: lito: WCN3990 Bluetooth via hci_qca

On Android the vendor bt_power driver powers the WCN3990 and the BT HAL
talks HCI over the UART from userspace. Use the in-kernel hci_qca serdev
driver instead, so BlueZ gets hci0: add a "qcom,wcn3990-bt" child to the
qupv3 SE5 UART with the supplies hci_qca expects, mapped to the same
regulators the vendor node uses. The vendor node stays enabled because
the sound card depends on the BT FM SLIMbus codec, which needs it. Needs
BT_HCIUART_QCA and the firmware links from bramble-firmware.

---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/vendor/qcom/lito.dtsi	2026-10-23 16:22:08.152399844 +0100
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/vendor/qcom/lito.dtsi	2026-10-23 16:22:08.154116348 +0100
@@ -3955,3 +3955,23 @@
 };
 #include "lito-sde.dtsi"
 #include "lito-sde-pll.dtsi"
+
+/*
+ * WCN3990 Bluetooth on the qupv3 SE5 UART, driven by the in-kernel hci_qca
+ * serdev driver (compatible "qcom,wcn3990-bt", drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c)
+ * so BlueZ gets hci0. The supplies are the regulators the vendor
+ * "bluetooth" node (bt_wcn3990) uses; regulators are reference-counted, so
+ * both can hold them. The vendor node must stay enabled: the BT FM
+ * SLIMbus codec (btfmslim) needs bt_power, and without that codec the
+ * sound card never probes. This lives in the base DT because the stock
+ * bramble dtbo does not touch these nodes.
+ */
+&qupv3_se5_4uart {
+	bluetooth {
+		compatible = "qcom,wcn3990-bt";
+		vddio-supply = <&pm8150_s4>;
+		vddxo-supply = <&pm8150_l14>;
+		vddrf-supply = <&pm8150a_l2>;
+		vddch0-supply = <&pm8150a_l10>;
+	};
+};
